  "correction_rules": [
    "Overall positive bias: model forecasts are typically ~0.2–1.5 €/t too high versus realized prices across most horizons; apply a small downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Overshoot at medium/long horizons (≈day 15–30): the model tends to keep prices elevated or rising when outcomes mean-revert/soften; dampen trend beyond ~2 weeks (shrink deviations from the recent mean by ~20–40%).",
    "Spike persistence error: after upward moves in history, the model extrapolates the spike too strongly; cap post-spike upside and force faster reversion toward the 10–20d average.",
    "Late-horizon drawdown miss: when the model projects a sharp selloff into the last week, realized prices often fall less and rebound sooner; reduce the magnitude of projected late declines (compress negative moves by ~20–30%).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias shape: near-term (day 1–7) errors are smaller and can be slightly low at times, but day 8–25 is consistently too high; use a bias that becomes more negative with horizon (e.g., 0 at day 1–3, then increasingly −0.5 to −1.0 €/t by day 20+)."
  ]
